{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The thematic content of students' open-ended responses -- i.e. what topics/themes they raise and their valence.",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "themes",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Multi-label decomposition of each response into a coherent taxonomy of themes (hands_on_missing, digital_tools_engagement, teacher_quality, etc.), directly realising the 'different themes' focus at a rich granularity."
    },
    {
      "name": "primary_theme",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Single dominant theme per response using the same coherent taxonomy; complements the multi-label column and enables frequency/ranking analysis of the leading theme."
    },
    {
      "name": "sentiment",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Valence facet (positive/mixed/negative) that adds an interpretive dimension to the themes, letting an analyst cross themes with tone rather than being a bare mechanical restatement."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.9,
  "structure_quality": 0.85,
  "focus_actionability": 0.85,
  "rationale": "All three columns centre tightly on one inferable focus: the themes present in student open responses. 'themes' and 'primary_theme' share a coherent, substantive taxonomy (multi-label + dominant-label views), giving a sound analytical structure at useful granularity rather than a single restatement. 'sentiment' is a defensible added facet that enriches theme analysis (theme-by-valence) rather than being noise, though on its own bare sentiment would be weak. The implied focus is genuinely actionable -- an analyst can rank themes, examine co-occurrence, and correlate with satisfaction/role/grade. Minor deductions: sentiment is somewhat generic, and no columns tie themes to the existing satisfaction/grade structure, which would sharpen actionability further."
}
